---
import { PALETTES } from "../../../packages/appearance/src/palettes.ts"
import { agentList } from "../agents.ts"
import { OTHERS, READ_ON, ROWS, sourcesOf } from "../compare.ts"
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro"
import { siteMap } from "../sitemap.ts"
import { TRACKING_ISSUE, UPCOMING, issueUrl } from "../upcoming.ts"
import "../styles/home.css"

const plugins = (await siteMap()).groups.find((group) => group.title === "Plugins")
if (plugins === undefined) throw new Error('docs/index.md has no "## Plugins" group')
const light = PALETTES.filter((palette) => palette.scheme === "light").length
const dark = PALETTES.length - light
---

<Base
  title="olai: Markdown notes and outlines in plain files, with the AI agent you choose"
  description="olai is an open-source app for Markdown notes and outlines that you run on your own computer. You edit them in the browser. An AI agent that you choose can edit the same files with you."
>
<main>

<div class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="kicker">open source · plain files · the AI agent you choose</p>
        <div class="brand">
          <h1>olai</h1>
          <img class="leafmark" src="/leaf.jpg" width="1024" height="1365" alt="A palm leaf drawn in ink, with coral tips. It is the olai mark.">
        </div>
        <p class="tag">Markdown notes and outlines that you and an AI agent edit together.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-say">
        <p class="sub">olai opens a directory of your notes in the browser. The
        directory holds two kinds of file. A document is a Markdown file, the
        same kind of file that Obsidian keeps. An outline is a list of items,
        and an item can hold more items under it. You edit both, and an AI
        agent does the same when you ask it.</p>
        <p class="sub">Do you keep a folder of Markdown notes? olai opens it as
        it is. Some things that Obsidian does are
        <a href="#upcoming">upcoming for Markdown</a>, and wikilinks are one of
        them.</p>
        <p class="next">
          <a class="go" href="#see">Watch the recording</a>
          <a href="#try">What you need to run olai</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot crop">
      <img src="/tour/outline-and-document.png" width="2880" height="1800" alt="olai in a browser. The sidebar on the left lists two outlines and a section of documents named Reference. The middle pane shows the outline Kitchen renovation. The right pane shows the Markdown document Cabinets, with its properties, its headings, and a table.">
    </figure>
    <p class="caption only-wide">The real app. The middle pane shows an outline. The right pane shows a Markdown document that an item of the outline links to.</p>
    <p class="caption only-narrow">A close view of the real app. This part shows a Markdown document. An outline is open beside it.</p>

    <ul class="points three">
      <li><b>Your notes are plain files.</b>
        <span>olai keeps them in a directory, which is a folder on your computer. A document is a Markdown file. An outline is a text file with one item per line, written as JSON. If you stop using olai, the files stay where they are.</span></li>
      <li><b>You choose the AI agent.</b>
        <span>olai has no AI of its own. It runs {agentList} with the account that you already have. You pick the agent each time you start a chat.</span></li>
      <li><b>You see every change.</b>
        <span>The agent works in a chat beside the outline. Each change appears on the page when the agent makes it. olai records your changes and the agent's changes in git, so you can read them later.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="honest"><b>What it takes today.</b> olai is new. It runs on a Mac or a Linux computer, and you start it from a terminal with Nix. olai has no phone app and no account to create. Read <a href="#try">what you need to run olai</a>.</p>

    <nav class="chapters" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#see">the recording</a>
      <a href="#kinds">documents and outlines</a>
      <a href="#agent">AI agents</a>
      <a href="#compare">comparison</a>
      <a href="#history">git history</a>
      <a href="#agenda">what is due</a>
      <a href="#find">filter and search</a>
      <a href="#plugins">plugins</a>
      <a href="#uses">uses</a>
      <a href="#try">run olai</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</div>

<section id="see">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Watch an agent mark an item done</h2>
    <p class="lead">The recording is 29 seconds long and has no sound.</p>
    <video class="tour" controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster="/tour/tour-poster.png" width="1024" height="640">
      <source src="/tour/tour.webm" type="video/webm">
      <source src="/tour/tour.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    </video>
    <p class="caption">You add an item, start an agent on the outline, and ask the agent to mark an item done. The grey text under your first message is the instruction that olai gives the agent. The agent in the recording is scripted.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="kinds">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Write Markdown documents and outlines in one directory</h2>
    <p class="lead">olai has two kinds of file, and each kind is a whole part
    of the app. Both kinds have pages, links, search, and agents.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="steps3">
        <div>
          <h3>A document is a Markdown file</h3>
          <p>olai draws headings, tables, task lists, footnotes, code, and
          pictures. A standard Markdown link opens another document, a
          heading, or an item of an outline. <strong>Referenced by</strong>
          lists what links to the document. The frontmatter at the top of the
          file becomes properties, and search finds them.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>An outline is a list of items</h3>
          <p>An outline is for work that has structure, such as a project, a
          plan, or a list of to-dos. An item has a mark, a date, and a note.
          You indent an item, move it, and make it repeat.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>The two kinds link to each other</h3>
          <p>An item links to a document, and a document links to an item.
          Each one lists what links to it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/document.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="A Markdown document in olai, with two headings, a table, a task list, and the item that links to the document."></div>
        <figcaption class="caption">The real app. A document, with a table, a task list, and the item that links to it.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="upcoming" id="upcoming">
      <h3>Upcoming for Markdown</h3>
      <p>olai does not do these things yet. Each one has an issue that you can follow.</p>
      <ul>
        {UPCOMING.map((entry) => (
          <li><a href={issueUrl(entry.issue)} set:html={entry.words} /><Fragment set:html={entry.after ?? "."} /></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>One issue lists <a href={issueUrl(TRACKING_ISSUE)}>every gap between a document and an outline</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="agent">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Bring your own AI agent</h2>
    <p class="lead">olai has no AI of its own, and it sells none. It runs an
    agent that you already use: {agentList}. The agent signs in with your
    account.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="steps3">
        <div>
          <h3>Ask on any item or document</h3>
          <p>Start a chat on an item or on a document, and write what you want.
          The agent can add items, mark them done, move them, give them dates,
          search your outlines, and write documents.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Keep working while the agent works</h3>
          <p>Each item shows what its agent is doing. When an agent needs an
          answer from you, its item shows <strong>Needs you</strong>. The
          sidebar lists those items under <strong>Needs you</strong> too.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Change the agent, or leave olai</h3>
          <p>You pick the agent each time you start a chat. If you stop using
          olai, your outlines and documents are still files in your directory.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What an agent needs</h3>
          <p>These agents are made for programmers. Each agent needs an account
          with the company that makes it. olai includes what it needs to run
          Claude Code and Codex. You install OpenCode, Pi, or Oh My Pi
          yourself. A program that uses MCP can connect to the same outlines.
          MCP is a standard way for AI programs to call tools.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <figure>
          <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/outline-and-chat.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="An outline beside a chat. In the chat an agent has marked four items of the outline done."></div>
          <figcaption class="caption">The real app. An outline, and a chat with an agent about it. The agent has marked four items done. The agent in this picture is scripted, so that the picture can be made again when olai changes.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="file">house.olai</div>
          <div class="row"><span class="bullet">●</span><span class="t"><strong>Kitchen renovation</strong> <span class="rollup">3/5</span></span></div>
          <div class="row d1"><span class="box doing"></span><span class="t">Order the new cabinets <span class="tagchip">#blocking</span><span class="agentmark needs">Needs you</span></span></div>
          <div class="row d1"><span class="box done"></span><span class="t"><span class="strike">Book the plumber</span><span class="agentmark">Idle</span></span></div>
          <div class="row d1"><span class="box"></span><span class="t">Meet the tiler on site<span class="agentmark works">Working…</span></span></div>
          <p class="caption">Three items have agents. One agent waits for you.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="compare">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>How olai compares with Obsidian, Logseq, Roam Research, and Workflowy</h2>
    <p class="lead">Where olai is behind, the table says so.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="cmp">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>olai</th>{OTHERS.map((other) => <th>{other.name}</th>)}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr><td>{row.label}</td><td>{row.olai.text}</td>{OTHERS.map((other) => <td>{row[other.key].text}</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="caption only-narrow">Swipe the table sideways to see Obsidian, Logseq, Roam Research, and Workflowy.</p>
    <p class="caption">Each fact about another product comes from that product's own website or repository, read on {READ_ON}. The sources are listed under the table.</p>
    <details class="sources">
      <summary>Sources for the table</summary>
      <ul>
        {OTHERS.map((other) => (
          <li>{other.name}: {sourcesOf(other.key).map((url, i) => (
            <>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={url}>{url.replace(/^https:\/\//, "").replace(/\/$/, "")}</a></>
          ))}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </details>

    <div class="fit">
      <div class="no">
        <h3>When olai is the wrong choice</h3>
        <p>olai is the wrong choice today if one of these is true.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>You want an app on your phone.</li>
          <li>You do not want to use a terminal.</li>
          <li>You use Windows.</li>
          <li>Your notes depend on something in <a href="#upcoming">the upcoming list for Markdown</a>.</li>
          <li>You depend on Obsidian's Canvas or on its community plugins.</li>
          <li>You need your notes to sync between devices without git.</li>
          <li>You need software that rarely changes. A new olai release can break things.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="yes">
        <h3>When olai may suit you</h3>
        <p>olai may suit you if one of these is true.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>You keep a folder of Markdown notes, and you want an AI agent to work on them.</li>
          <li>You organize projects, plans, and to-dos as outlines.</li>
          <li>You want an AI agent to edit your notes, and you want to choose the agent.</li>
          <li>You want files that you can open without olai.</li>
          <li>You want a record of every change.</li>
          <li>You can follow a setup that uses a terminal one time.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="history">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>olai records every change in git</h2>
    <p class="lead">olai saves each edit to your files at once. olai also
    records the changes in git. Programmers use git to keep the history of
    their files.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <figure>
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/commit-panel.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The commit panel, open over an outline and a chat. It lists four items in house.olai marked done and one item edited, and a button that reads Commit 5 changes."></div>
        <figcaption class="caption">The real app. The commit panel lists each change in plain words, such as <strong>Done</strong>. The agent in this picture is scripted.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p>The commit panel lists the changes that wait to be recorded. It
        describes each change in plain words, such as <em>done</em> or
        <em>created</em>.</p>
        <p>You record the changes when you choose. You can also set olai to
        record them 15 seconds after the last change.</p>
        <p>Each recorded change names what wrote it: the page that you typed
        in, or an agent's chat.</p>
        <p>To keep a copy on another computer, push the git history to GitHub
        or to another git server.</p>
        <p>You set git up one time, with one command on the
        <a href="/docs/start/">Start page</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="agenda">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>See what is due</h2>
    <p class="lead">The <strong>Agenda</strong> page lists the dated items from
    every outline. Late items are at the top.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <figure>
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/agenda.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Agenda page. Order the new cabinets is 5 days late. Book the plumber is due today. Pay the deposit is due in 3 days, and Meet the tiler on site is due in 2 weeks."></div>
        <figcaption class="caption">The real app. One item is late, one is due today, and two are due on later days.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p>A birthday is not a task. It stays on its day, and olai never marks
        it late.</p>
        <p>Each day has a page of its own. A document named for the day, such
        as <code>2026-08-12.md</code>, appears at the top of that day's page.</p>
        <p>An item that repeats every week comes back as a new item on its next
        date.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="find">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Filter an outline, or search every file</h2>
    <p class="lead">Type in the <strong>Filter</strong> box above an outline.
    olai hides the items that do not match. A click on a tag filters the same
    way.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="file">house.olai · filter: #home OR alcove</div>
        <div class="row"><span class="bullet">●</span><span class="t"><strong>kitchen remodel</strong> <span class="tagchip"><span class="hit">#home</span></span></span></div>
        <div class="row d1"><span class="bullet">●</span><span class="t">order the new cabinets</span></div>
        <div class="noteline">…measure the <span class="hit">alcove</span> before ordering.</div>
        <div class="row d1 dimrow"><span class="bullet">●</span><span class="t">install the cabinets</span></div>
        <div class="row d2"><span class="box"></span><span class="t">pick the hinges <span class="tagchip"><span class="hit">#home</span></span></span></div>
        <p class="caption">An item that matches stays under the items that it belongs to.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>The filter is part of the page's address. You can send the address
        to someone, or pin it in the sidebar.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Search</strong> box in the header searches every
        outline and document. Read <a href="/docs/search/">how to write a search</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="plugins">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Ask an agent for a plugin of your own</h2>
    <p class="lead">Describe the plugin in one sentence. An agent writes it
    while you watch. Read what the plugin does, and then click
    <strong>Turn on</strong>.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <figure>
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/plugins-panel.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Plugins panel. In the Agents group, Chat and Claude Code are switched on, and Codex, OpenCode, Pi, and Oh My Pi are switched off."></div>
        <figcaption class="caption">The real app. The <strong>Plugins</strong> panel, with the <strong>Agents</strong> group open. Each plugin has a switch.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p>A plugin can do everything that olai can do on your computer. olai
        does not run a plugin until you click <strong>Turn on</strong>.</p>
        <p>Most parts of olai are plugins with a switch, such as the calendar,
        search, and each agent. Switch off the parts that you do not use.</p>
        <p>A plugin that you asked for is a folder in your directory, so it
        moves with your notes. Its page says in plain words what the plugin
        does.</p>
        <p>The docs list <a href={`/docs/#${plugins.slug}`}>every plugin that olai includes</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="uses">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Use olai for Markdown notes, agent tasks, due dates, and quick capture</h2>
    <p class="lead">Each picture shows the real app.</p>
    <div class="teasers">
      <a href="/uses/#notes">
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/document.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
        <b>Keep Markdown notes</b>
        <span>Each document has a page, with its headings and what links to it.</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/uses/#give">
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/outline-and-chat.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
        <b>Give a task to an agent</b>
        <span>Each item and each document can have its own agent.</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/uses/#due">
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/agenda.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
        <b>See what is due</b>
        <span>The Agenda page lists the dated items from every outline. Late items are at the top.</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/uses/#capture">
        <div class="shot small"><img src="/tour/capture.png" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
        <b>Capture a thought</b>
        <span>One command opens a box. The item that you type goes to the inbox.</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="try">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Run olai on a Mac or a Linux computer</h2>
    <p class="lead">olai is free, and you do not create an account. You need
    these things to run it today.</p>
    <div class="try">
      <div>
        <ul>
          <li>A Mac or a Linux computer. olai does not run on Windows.</li>
          <li>A terminal.</li>
          <li><a href="https://nixos.org/download/">Nix</a>, a free program that downloads olai and runs it.</li>
          <li>An account with {agentList}, if you want an agent.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="next"><a class="go" href="/docs/start/">Set up olai and write your first line</a></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>This command starts olai on the directory <code>~/notes</code>. If the directory already holds Markdown files, olai serves them as they are.</p>
        <pre class="run" id="install" title="click to copy"><code>nix run github:juspay/olai -- web ~/notes</code></pre>
        <p class="caption">The Start page has every step, with the two commands that come before this one.</p>
        <p class="caption">olai has no installer for people who do not use a terminal, and no hosted version. A new olai release can break things. Keep your notes in git, as the Start page shows.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cols small">
      <div>
        <h3>Keys</h3>
        <p>olai uses the keys of other outliners. <code>Enter</code> starts the
        next item, and <code>Tab</code> indents it. The editing docs list
        <a href="/docs/editing/">every key</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Colours</h3>
        <p>olai has {PALETTES.length} colour palettes, {light} light and {dark}
        dark. Click a palette name, and this page changes to it. The round button in the header
        does the same on every page.</p>
        <div class="chips">
          {PALETTES.map((palette) => (
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-theme={palette.name} data-pick={palette.name}>{palette.name}</button>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</main>

<script is:inline>
  const install = document.getElementById("install");
  if (install) {
    install.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const text = install.textContent.replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
        install.title = "copied";
      } catch {
        install.title = "select and copy";
      }
    });
  }
</script>
</Base>
